  "segments": [
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Back in the dining room, the French guests are singing La Marseillaise. Dmitry Nagiyev looks at the drunk chef Jacques and decides to taste the dish himself. We'll hear it in Russian first; don't worry about catching every word.",
      "gap": 0.8
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Исполняют Марсельезу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Какой приятный вечер.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Давайте извиним Жак.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Он, видимо, сильно накушался.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Даже без нашего блюда.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Он очень устал сегодня.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Поэтому он не может есть.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ну что ж.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Тогда дегустатором буду я.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я сам попробую это.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Надеюсь, это вкусно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Посмотрим, что приготовил повар.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "Let's go through it line by line. Dmitry Nagiyev watches the singing guests.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Исполняют Марсельезу.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'They're singing La Marseillaise.' Nagiyev delivers a punchy one-word reaction built around the Russian word meaning 'to perform'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Какой приятный вечер.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'What a pleasant evening.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be' here, linking 'nice' directly with 'evening', combining 'nice' with 'evening'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Давайте извиним Жак.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Let's forgive Jacques.' The key word here means 'let's', which Dmitry combines with 'to excuse'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Он, видимо, сильно накушался.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'He seems to have had too much.' The verb for 'got drunk' is in the masculine past tense, marked by that 'l' sound at the end.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Даже без нашего блюда.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Even before our dish.' Nagiyev delivers a punchy one-word reaction built around the Russian word meaning 'of dish'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Он очень устал сегодня.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'He is very tired today.' Here Nagiyev anchors the line on the word meaning 'very much' and builds the thought around 'am tired' and 'today'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Поэтому он не может есть.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Therefore he cannot eat.' The phrasing opens with 'that's why' before bringing in 'can' and 'there is' to make the point clear.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Ну что ж.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Well, then.' Russian omits the present-tense verb 'to be' here, linking 'oh' directly with 'what', combining 'oh' with 'what' and 'on earth'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Тогда дегустатором буду я.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Then I will be the taster.' Nagiyev puts the word for 'back then' together with 'taster' and 'will be' in a crisp, natural spoken turn of phrase.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Я сам попробую это.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I will try it myself.' Nagiyev delivers a punchy one-word reaction built around the Russian word meaning 'will try'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Надеюсь, это вкусно.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'I hope it's tasty.' Nagiyev chooses the imperfective verb meaning 'hope so' for the ongoing action, followed by 'it's tasty'.",
      "gap": 0.4
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Посмотрим, что приготовил повар.",
      "gap": 0.55
    },
    {
      "v": "narrator",
      "t": "'Let's see what the chef cooked.' The verb for 'cooked' is in the masculine past tense, marked by the '-l' ending. Now let's listen to the whole conversation again from start to finish.",
      "gap": 0.9
    },
    {
      "v": "anya",
      "t": "Исполняют Марсельезу.",
      "gap": 0.5
    },
    {
